+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-applypatch-rfc-6902
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch applyPatch (RFC 6902)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Implement JSON Patch applyPatch (RFC 6902)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. Implement JSON Patch, as specified
+in RFC 6902, with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`.
+
+Export a function `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a JSON Patch (an array
+of operations) to a JSON document and returns the resulting document as a new value: it must not
+mutate its input. It throws an error when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails.
+
+It builds on `resolvePointer` (task-001, RFC 6901), which resolves the `path` and `from` pointers.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- Each operation is an object with an `op` member among `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+  and `test`; `path` is a JSON Pointer; `value` is required by `add`, `replace` and `test`; `from` is
+  required by `move` and `copy`. A missing or unknown `op`, a missing required member, or a patch that
+  is not an array throws.
+- `add`: inserts `value` at `path`. For an object, sets the member (replacing an existing one). For an
+  array, `-` appends; otherwise the index must be in `0..length` and the element is inserted before the
+  index. The empty path replaces the whole document.
+- `remove`: removes the member or element at `path`; the target must exist.
+- `replace`: sets the value at `path`; the target must exist. The empty path replaces the whole document.
+- `move`: removes the value at `from` and adds it at `path`. `from` must not be a proper prefix of
+  `path` (a location cannot be moved into one of its own children).
+- `copy`: adds a copy of the value at `from` to `path`.
+- `test`: succeeds when the value at `path` equals `value` (deep equality); otherwise it throws.
+- Operations apply in order, each to the result of the previous one. Any failing operation makes the
+  whole call throw, and the input is never modified.
+- The result is a new value: no structure shared with the input is mutated by later operations.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Library code in `src/index.ts`, no runtime dependency. Reuse `resolvePointer` for pointer parsing.
+- Tests with `node --test` covering each operation (happy path), the error paths above, RFC 6902
+  appendix examples, and that the input is not mutated.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- `applyPatch` and the types `JsonPatch` and `JsonPatchOperation` are exported from `src/index.ts`.
+  `resolvePointer` now shares its token walk with the patch code, so both read pointers the same way.
+- The patch is checked up front (array of objects, known `op`, `path` and the `value` or `from` member
+  that each operation needs); a `SyntaxError` is thrown before any operation runs. The document is
+  copied once, and every value taken from the patch is copied on insertion, so no input is mutated.
+- Members are set with `Object.defineProperty`, so a `__proto__` key stays a plain member.
+- Tests: `test/apply-patch.test.ts` (50 tests, written first and seen failing). `npm test` passes all
+  68 tests. The source type-checks under `tsc --strict` (checked with a temporary TypeScript install).
+- README documents the new function under "JSON Patch".
